    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Could you expand a bit more on the specific types of Amadori products that tend to form depending on the sugar used? Also, in a professional kitchen setting, what are some practical ways a chef might actually manipulate the pH of a protein—like shellfish or beef—to ensure the Maillard reaction is optimized without causing issues with texture or safety?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Amadori products are a diverse group, and the specific sugar dictates the initial structure of the intermediate, though they all generally fall under this class. For instance, when using hexoses like glucose, the resulting Amadori products are specific ketosamines. The exact structure depends on which amino acid is involved in the initial condensation.\n\nIn a professional kitchen, manipulating the pH of proteins like beef or shellfish is tricky, as you don't want to cook them into a mushy state. Chefs often use buffers or mild acid/base additions, such as incorporating a splash of baking soda (a mild base) or using specific marinades that are slightly alkaline, to gently elevate the surface pH just before searing. This slight shift encourages the Maillard reaction on the exterior crust without drastically altering the interior texture.",
      "source": "target",
